  {
   "id": "intro",
   "type": "intro",
   "heading": {
    "en": "Thinking for yourself"
   },
   "hook": {
    "en": "There is a day when you realise that nobody else can do your thinking for you. Is that the day childhood ends?"
   },
   "objectives": [
    {
     "en": "Explain the third possibility"
    },
    {
     "en": "Understand individuality"
    },
    {
     "en": "Connect the three possibilities as stages of growing up"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "There is a day when you realise that nobody else can do your thinking for you. Is that the day childhood ends?"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "concept1",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "Stanza 3: individuality"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "In the third stanza the speaker asks whether childhood ended when he discovered that his mind was really his own. He could use it in any way he chose, and his thoughts belonged to him and to no one else. This discovery of individuality means he no longer simply accepts what others say; he thinks and judges for himself."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "His mind is his own"
    },
    {
     "en": "He can use it as he chooses"
    },
    {
     "en": "His thoughts belong to him alone"
    },
    {
     "en": "Individuality: thinking and deciding for oneself"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "In the third stanza the speaker asks whether childhood ended when he discovered that his mind was really his own. He could use it in any way he chose, and his thoughts belonged to him and to no one else. This discovery of individuality means he no longer simply accepts what others say; he thinks and judges for himself."
   }
